A||||6||MrFieroUK||Modifications.||||1||
Z||000000||MrFieroUK||11-02-2001||01:58 PM||||BACKGOUND.<BR>It had to happen... Just over 15 months ago I helped my son import a 1987 GT into the UK from the Netherlands - his first American car at the age of 18 - quite an acheivment for a young guy in Britain - even the insurance was reasonable. Any how, I have recently inherited it (purchased it back off my son so he can play around in European hot hatches/GTIs  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> - silly boy) you guessed - I got to driving it and got hooked on this little car's character and the amount of attraction it gets. As if I needed another car... I have 4 Lincolns, a Chrysler and an AMC...<P>CURRENT.<BR>However, It hasn't taken me long to get fed up with this car's shortcomings. First out was the front brakes - now running 9.7 inch vented rotors, modified hubs and Grand Am calipers - plus a Blazer/CK master cylinder with a larger bore. At the moment I am gathering together the parts for the rear -Grand Am front vented rotors and calipers and Wilwood mechanical spot calipers for the e-brake plus some special fabrication for brackets etc.<P>That little V6 is sweet but gets really breathless above 5 grand, especially since I put a fresh timing gear/chain/damper assembly on it and lost the slack!<P>PROBLEMS.<BR>Here's the deal - I've got the Wizzard at Lazer cams to spec up a grind profile for hydraulic roller tappets (power band 2000 to 62000+ rpm.) and supply all valve train parts except for valves and roller followers - I will order next week. Thought about using crane 1.6 roller rockers. Also deliberating on a 3.1 stroker kit with dished pistons, I therefore want to use aluminum splayed valve heads (with the smaller 28cc chambers) and assume I will need a new lower intake manifold to match intake port configuration.<BR>1.) Does any one know if the intermediate manifold and fuel rail assy. etc. will bolt right up to the new lower manifold. What about accessory bracket bolt patterns on the new heads?<BR>2.) Gas in the UK is currently around 71 pence a liter (roughly $3.60 per US gallon) and the Fiero is already way thirstier than my 1989 Lncoln Mark VII LSC with a 5.0 HO V8. I'm lazy and prefer a slush box - so the obvious route is replace the sweet 'lil THM125 with a suitably modified THM440T4.<BR>I need to find out what will be required to adapt the 440T4 to my existing cradle/driveshafts/bellhousing etc. I'd assume a 440T4 off a GEN II 2.8 or 3.1 might have the appropriate specs - I want a 3.33 final drive or higher - anyone care to comment?<BR>3.) Has anyone gone down this route(s) already and know of the pitfalls/problems to be aware of - with fall almost over, it is only a matter of a few weeks before I drop the rear cradle and start work - any help would be gratefully appreciated. Thanx.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Old Lar||11-02-2001||03:41 PM||||I have a 3.4L with a t440 automatic which was used in the early 1990's GM cars with a 3800 V6.  The 3.4L was a crate engine with no performance modifications.  Twin Lakes Fiero (see vendor list) did the conversion for me.  There were some axle modifications done, and aside from moving the starter, I think most everything bolted together and fit into the Fiero cradle.  My car is set for highway cruising at 80 mph, the rpms are at 2000, while getting some 30 mpg.<P>My suggestion would be to email Bob at Twin Lakes or Ed Parks at the Fiero Factory or some of the others in the vendor list that do lots of various conversions for more information.  What you are planning is over this old bald head.||||reg||1
Z||000002||ray b||11-02-2001||03:53 PM||||GET A COMPLEAT 3.4 WITH TRANS may be cheaper than buying parts if alloy heads are used you lose the distrubuter and need to rewire in a new CPU for the 3.4 with crank triger.<BR>DOHC 3.4 will allso fit and is about the same work to swap as the push-rod with alloy heads PLUS MORE POWER and RPM.<BR>USE THE SEARCH ON 3.4 MODS FOR MORE INFO<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000003||Dave Gunsul||11-02-2001||11:55 PM||||You can't use the aluminum heads on the Fiero without changing the manifold to the<BR>other cars manifolds that fits the heads, such as the Barretta, Z24, etc. This also means that once you use that manifold the distributor from the Fiero won't fit anymore.<BR>(the newer intake runs right into where it would normally go) That means that you would also have to convert over to the DIS ignition<BR>system as well.<BR>As you can see this is a lot of hassle to lose a little weight from the aluminum heads,<BR>they really don't flow much better either.<BR>I would suggest, in addition to your engine<BR>plans, to start uncorking the exhaust and porting the manifolds that will make the V6 <BR>breath much better on the high end as well as the low.<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
Z||000004||MrFieroUK||11-03-2001||06:31 AM||||Thanks for the tips guys. I'll let you know which way I go- DIS doesn't really bother me, but I do want to keep the stock underhood look - its just a real classic "Detroit Iron" class view!Now what about the driveshafts on that 440T4?||||reg||14
